WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.683
Can an input stop a spike without moving a resting cell's voltage at all?

2
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:10.133
Our cell starts at rest.

3
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:16.873
Alone, the 5 nS excitatory input would carry it toward minus −43 past threshold.

4
00:00:17.853 --> 00:00:18.893
It would fire on the way.

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:22.787
Now meet an inhibitory rope of 10 nS.

6
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:27.187
It is tied exactly at rest, minus −65 mV.

7
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.590
First, what does this rope do to a resting cell?

8
00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:42.043
Nothing. Its rope is tied exactly where the marker sits, so it pulls on nothing.

9
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.523
The trace stays at minus −65

10
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:50.033
Now start again from rest, and switch on the excitation and the shunt together.

11
00:00:50.933 --> 00:00:51.953
Where does the marker head?

12
00:00:57.933 --> 00:01:00.093
Toward minus −52 mV.

13
00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:03.333
That is below threshold, so the cell never fires.

14
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:08.133
The shunt's top-line term is zero, so it adds nothing to the top.

15
00:01:08.953 --> 00:01:11.653
It acts through the bottom line, the total conductance.

16
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:16.387
The depolarization falls from about 22 mV to 13

17
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:18.907
This is shunting inhibition.

18
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:21.147
It moves nothing at rest.

19
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:25.237
It shows itself when something else tries to move the voltage.

20
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:27.117
Then it cuts that movement.

21
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.370
The shunt is a rope tied where the marker already sits.

22
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.310
It holds the marker back whenever another rope drags it away.

23
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.240
Electrically, the shunt lowers input resistance.

24
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.100
Excitation moves the voltage less, whether it is a synaptic conductance or injected

25
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.100
current.

26
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:48.383
It changed only the bottom line.

27
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:51.053
The shunt does a second thing.

28
00:01:51.873 --> 00:01:53.273
It shortens the time constant.

29
00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:57.997
Now switch off the excitation, and let the cell return to rest.

30
00:01:58.877 --> 00:02:01.657
With the leak and the shunt open, τ is 10 ms.

31
00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:03.917
That is half the leak's 20

32
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.240
That is cell B from the summation chapter.

33
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:11.473
Give it two brief pulses again, 10 ms apart.

34
00:02:12.413 --> 00:02:14.993
Less of the first is left for the second to ride on.

35
00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:19.373
In this idealized case, each jump stays the same size.

36
00:02:20.253 --> 00:02:21.253
Only the tail is shorter.

37
00:02:22.317 --> 00:02:25.677
A real synaptic current flows for a millisecond or more.

38
00:02:26.357 --> 00:02:29.777
While it flows, the shunt drains charge, so the peak is smaller too.

39
00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:33.487
A shunt tied at rest moves nothing at rest.

40
00:02:34.227 --> 00:02:37.387
It shows itself as smaller EPSPs and less summation.
